</span>
                Science for a risky world—A U.S. Geological Survey plan for risk research and applications

              </a>
          </h4>
    
<div class="field field--name--field-intro field--type--text-long field--label--hidden">
  
      <div  class="field-intro">  Executive Summary Natural hazards—including earthquakes, tsunamis, volcanic eruptions, landslides, hurricanes, droughts, floods, wildfires, geomagnetic storms, and pandemics—can wreak havoc on human communities, the economy, and natural resources for years following an initial event. Hazards can claim lives and cause billions of dollars in damage to homes and infrastructure as well as...

</div>
  
</div>

        
    
    

    
